<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.1" schemaVersion="1.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>BrowseCraft</displayName>
  <description>BrowseCraft's own settings.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="BrowseCraft">BrowseCraft</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_BrowseCraft155">BrowseCraft 155 and later</string>
      <string id="SyncServerUrl">Sync server (sign in with the Windows account)</string>
      <string id="SyncServerUrl_Explain">The address of your organisation's BrowseCraft server, for example http://browsecraft.example.org:8295. Use the server's DNS name (the one in its Kerberos SPN, HTTP/browsecraft.example.org), not an IP address.

When this is set, BrowseCraft signs each profile in to that server with the Windows account of whoever is logged on, when it starts, if the server has single sign-on set up. Nobody types a password. Bookmarks, passwords, history, open tabs, extensions and settings then sync, and the profile takes the user's full name from the directory.

If that does not work (the PC is not on the domain, or the server has no single sign-on), people can sign in from the profile menu (Sign in) with a user name and password. Someone who signs out is not signed in again automatically, until they sign in themselves.

When it is not set, the Extension store address is used for this too; with neither, people sign in themselves on chrome://browsecraft.

Set it for computers (applies to everyone on them) or for users; the computer setting wins. BrowseCraft reads it when it starts.</string>
      <string id="ExtensionStoreUrl">Extension store (your BrowseCraft server)</string>
      <string id="ExtensionStoreUrl_Explain">The address of your organisation's BrowseCraft server, for example https://browsecraft.example.org (the same server people sync to).

When this is set, BrowseCraft installs and updates extensions only from that server's store (its /webstore page): extensions your administrators have approved there. Extensions that came from the Chrome Web Store update from your server too, and the Chrome Web Store itself cannot install anything.

When it is not set, BrowseCraft uses the Chrome Web Store.

Set it for computers (applies to everyone on them) or for users; the computer setting wins. BrowseCraft reads it when it starts.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="SyncServerUrl">
        <textBox refId="SyncServerUrl">
          <label>Server address:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="ExtensionStoreUrl">
        <textBox refId="ExtensionStoreUrl">
          <label>Server address:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
